  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Express $\\frac{3x}{(x-1)(x+2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{3x}{(x-1)(x+2)}=\\,$ | $1$ | $+$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-1$ |  | $x+2$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x}{(x-1)(x+2)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n3x=(x+2)+B(x-1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2) & =(-2+2)+B(-2-1) \\\\ -6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x}{(x-1)(x+2)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{2}{x+2}.\n$$",
